WEBVTT

00:00.470 --> 00:03.160
Let's graph ourselves
some inequalities.

00:03.160 --> 00:06.050
So let's say I had the
inequality y is less than or

00:06.050 --> 00:11.520
equal to 4x plus 3.

00:11.520 --> 00:15.500
On our xy coordinate plane, we
want to show all the x and y

00:15.500 --> 00:19.650
points that satisfy this
condition right here.

00:19.650 --> 00:22.210
So a good starting point might
be to break up this less than

00:22.210 --> 00:24.570
or equal to, because we
know how to graph y is

00:24.570 --> 00:26.140
equal to 4x plus 3.

00:26.140 --> 00:31.510
So this thing is the same thing
as y could be less than

00:31.510 --> 00:38.870
4x plus 3, or y could be
equal to 4x plus 3.

00:38.870 --> 00:41.170
That's what less than
or equal means.

00:41.170 --> 00:43.200
It could be less
than or equal.

00:43.200 --> 00:46.015
And the reason why I did that on
this first example problem

00:46.015 --> 00:47.730
is because we know how
to graph that.

00:47.730 --> 00:48.980
So let's graph that.

00:52.060 --> 00:55.170
Try to draw a little bit
neater than that.

00:55.170 --> 00:57.800
So that is-- no, that's
not good.

00:57.800 --> 01:02.460
So that is my vertical
axis, my y-axis.

01:02.460 --> 01:07.920
This is my x-axis,
right there.

01:07.920 --> 01:09.920
And then we know the
y-intercept, the

01:09.920 --> 01:11.580
y-intercept is 3.

01:11.580 --> 01:16.510
So the point 0, 3-- 1, 2,
3-- is on the line.

01:16.510 --> 01:18.405
And we know we have
a slope of 4.

01:21.330 --> 01:25.430
Which means if we go 1 in the
x-direction, we're going to go

01:25.430 --> 01:26.980
up 4 in the y.

01:26.980 --> 01:29.280
So 1, 2, 3, 4.

01:29.280 --> 01:31.920
So it's going to
be right here.

01:31.920 --> 01:33.236
And that's enough
to draw a line.

01:33.236 --> 01:35.210
We could even go back
in the x-direction.

01:35.210 --> 01:37.290
If we go 1 back in the
x-direction, we're

01:37.290 --> 01:38.500
going to go down 4.

01:38.500 --> 01:40.930
1, 2, 3, 4.

01:40.930 --> 01:43.480
So that's also going to be
a point on the line.

01:43.480 --> 01:47.670
So my best attempt at drawing
this line is going to look

01:47.670 --> 01:52.590
something like-- this
is the hardest part.

01:52.590 --> 01:55.610
It's going to look something
like that.

01:55.610 --> 01:57.270
That is a line.

01:57.270 --> 01:58.470
It should be straight.

01:58.470 --> 02:00.240
I think you get the idea.

02:00.240 --> 02:05.540
That right there is the graph
of y is equal to 4x plus 3.

02:05.540 --> 02:07.440
So let's think about what it
means to be less than.

02:07.440 --> 02:09.789
So all of these points
satisfy this

02:09.789 --> 02:11.750
inequality, but we have more.

02:11.750 --> 02:13.410
This is just these
points over here.

02:13.410 --> 02:17.830
What about all these where
y ix less than 4x plus 3?

02:17.830 --> 02:20.790
So let's think about
what this means.

02:20.790 --> 02:22.410
Let's pick up some
values for x.

02:22.410 --> 02:26.140
When x is equal to 0,
what does this say?

02:26.140 --> 02:29.390
When x is equal to 0, then that
means y is going to be

02:29.390 --> 02:33.150
less than 0 plus 3.
y is less than 3.

02:33.150 --> 02:40.620
When x is equal to negative 1,
what is this telling us?

02:40.620 --> 02:44.590
4 times negative 1 is negative
4, plus 3 is negative 1. y

02:44.590 --> 02:46.840
would be less than negative 1.

02:46.840 --> 02:50.950
When x is equal to 1, what
is this telling us?

02:50.950 --> 02:54.110
4 times 1 is 4, plus 3 is 7.

02:54.110 --> 02:57.340
So y is going to
be less than 7.

02:57.340 --> 02:59.130
So let's at least try
to plot these.

02:59.130 --> 03:04.670
So when x is equal to-- let's
plot this one first. When x is

03:04.670 --> 03:07.805
equal to 0, y is less than 3.

03:11.350 --> 03:15.230
So it's all of these points
here-- that I'm shading in in

03:15.230 --> 03:19.310
green-- satisfy that
right there.

03:19.310 --> 03:22.200
If I were to look at this one
over here, when x is negative

03:22.200 --> 03:26.140
1, y is less than negative 1.

03:26.140 --> 03:30.850
So y has to be all of these
points down here.

03:30.850 --> 03:36.470
When x is equal to 1,
y is less than 7.

03:36.470 --> 03:39.500
So it's all of these
points down here.

03:39.500 --> 03:44.960
And in general, you take any
point x-- let's say you take

03:44.960 --> 03:47.020
this point x right there.

03:47.020 --> 03:50.980
If you evaluate 4x plus 3,
you're going to get the point

03:50.980 --> 03:52.120
on the line.

03:52.120 --> 03:55.950
That is that x times 4 plus 3.

03:55.950 --> 03:58.860
Now the y's that satisfy it,
it could be equal to that

03:58.860 --> 04:01.100
point on the line, or it
could be less than.

04:01.100 --> 04:03.840
So it's going to go
below the line.

04:03.840 --> 04:06.870
So if you were to do this for
all the possible x's, you

04:06.870 --> 04:11.130
would not only get all the
points on this line which

04:11.130 --> 04:15.990
we've drawn, you would get all
the points below the line.

04:15.990 --> 04:19.279
So now we have graphed
this inequality.

04:19.279 --> 04:23.480
It's essentially this line, 4x
plus 3, with all of the area

04:23.480 --> 04:25.490
below it shaded.

04:25.490 --> 04:29.530
Now, if this was just a less
than, not less than or equal

04:29.530 --> 04:32.670
sign, we would not include
the actual line.

04:32.670 --> 04:36.090
And the convention to do that is
to actually make the line a

04:36.090 --> 04:37.860
dashed line.

04:37.860 --> 04:44.880
This is the situation if we were
dealing with just less

04:44.880 --> 04:46.690
than 4x plus 3.

04:46.690 --> 04:49.410
Because in that situation, this
wouldn't apply, and we

04:49.410 --> 04:50.620
would just have that.

04:50.620 --> 04:53.550
So the line itself wouldn't have
satisfied it, just the

04:53.550 --> 04:55.010
area below it.

04:55.010 --> 04:57.190
Let's do one like that.

04:57.190 --> 05:06.230
So let's say we have y is
greater than negative x

05:06.230 --> 05:09.530
over 2 minus 6.

05:09.530 --> 05:11.940
So a good way to start-- the
way I like to start these

05:11.940 --> 05:14.830
problems-- is to just graph
this equation right here.

05:14.830 --> 05:18.780
So let me just graph-- just for
fun-- let me graph y is

05:18.780 --> 05:24.200
equal to-- this is the same
thing as negative 1/2 minus 6.

05:24.200 --> 05:30.890
So if we were to graph it, that
is my vertical axis, that

05:30.890 --> 05:34.130
is my horizontal axis.

05:34.130 --> 05:36.920
And our y-intercept
is negative 6.

05:36.920 --> 05:40.170
So 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

05:40.170 --> 05:42.020
So that's my y-intercept.

05:42.020 --> 05:43.960
And my slope is negative 1/2.

05:43.960 --> 05:49.390
Oh, that should be an x there,
negative 1/2 x minus 6.

05:49.390 --> 05:53.950
So my slope is negative 1/2,
which means when I go 2 to the

05:53.950 --> 05:55.395
right, I go down 1.

05:55.395 --> 05:58.775
So if I go 2 to the right,
I'm going to go down 1.

06:02.590 --> 06:05.870
If I go 2 to the left, if
I go negative 2, I'm

06:05.870 --> 06:06.880
going to go up 1.

06:06.880 --> 06:09.040
So negative 2, up 1.

06:09.040 --> 06:11.530
So my line is going
to look like this.

06:11.530 --> 06:15.200
My line is going to
look like that.

06:15.200 --> 06:17.400
That's my best attempt
at drawing the line.

06:17.400 --> 06:19.400
So that's the line
of y is equal to

06:19.400 --> 06:22.080
negative 1/2 x minus 6.

06:22.080 --> 06:24.730
Now, our inequality is not
greater than or equal, it's

06:24.730 --> 06:28.320
just greater than negative x
over 2 minus 6, or greater

06:28.320 --> 06:30.850
than negative 1/2 x minus 6.

06:30.850 --> 06:35.230
So using the same logic as
before, for any x-- so if you

06:35.230 --> 06:39.070
take any x, let's say that's
our particular x we want to

06:39.070 --> 06:43.260
pick-- if you evaluate negative
x over 2 minus 6,

06:43.260 --> 06:44.970
you're going to get that
point right there.

06:44.970 --> 06:47.720
You're going to get the
point on the line.

06:47.720 --> 06:50.620
But the y's that satisfy this
inequality are the y's

06:50.620 --> 06:51.650
greater than that.

06:51.650 --> 06:54.920
So it's going to be not that
point-- in fact, you draw an

06:54.920 --> 06:58.930
open circle there-- because you
can't include the point of

06:58.930 --> 07:00.750
negative 1/2 x minus 6.

07:00.750 --> 07:02.750
But it's going to be all the
y's greater than that.

07:05.300 --> 07:06.670
That'd be true for any x.

07:06.670 --> 07:07.900
You take this x.

07:07.900 --> 07:11.260
You evaluate negative 1/2 or
negative x over 2 minus 6,

07:11.260 --> 07:13.660
you're going to get this
point over here.

07:13.660 --> 07:19.100
The y's that satisfy it are
all the y's above that.

07:19.100 --> 07:22.660
So all of the y's that satisfy
this equation, or all of the

07:22.660 --> 07:25.990
coordinates that satisfy this
equation, is this entire area

07:25.990 --> 07:28.310
above the line.

07:28.310 --> 07:30.070
And we're not going to
include the line.

07:30.070 --> 07:33.300
So the convention is to make
this line into a dashed line.

07:33.300 --> 07:35.850
And let me draw-- I'm trying
my best to turn it into a

07:35.850 --> 07:36.430
dashed line.

07:36.430 --> 07:41.070
I'll just erase sections of
the line, and hopefully it

07:41.070 --> 07:43.160
will look dashed to you.

07:43.160 --> 07:45.590
So I'm turning that solid line
into a dashed line to show

07:45.590 --> 07:48.500
that it's just a boundary, but
it's not included in the

07:48.500 --> 07:51.210
coordinates that satisfy
our inequality.

07:51.210 --> 07:53.760
The coordinates that satisfy our
equality are all of this

07:53.760 --> 08:00.430
yellow stuff that I'm shading
above the line.

08:00.430 --> 08:03.260
Anyway, hopefully you
found that helpful.
